@page "/preview/terminal"

@* A development preview of a MAUI screen, not a back-office destination.

   The terminal is Restaurant.Mobile — Razor in an Android WebView. The same
   components render in a desktop browser, so this route stands them up at the
   artboard's 1440×900 and lets the screen be seen and iterated without an
   emulator on the machine. Nothing here is part of the product: the back office
   has no terminal, and this page never ships to a venue.

   Two things the preview must not lie about, because a desktop browser is not
   the device:

   · Hover. The kit declares every :hover rule inside one @@media (hover:hover)
     guard, so a pointer surface gets the lift and the Android terminal never
     does. That guard is correct and this browser satisfies it, which means the
     preview would show a lift the device cannot produce. The frame below
     retracts it, so what you see is what the terminal does: no transform, and
     press feedback as the ripple tint (§8).
   · Size. The frame is a fixed 1440×900 box and does not scale to the window.
     A scaled preview reads as a smaller terminal and hides how much a 190px
     tile actually is; scroll the frame instead.

   The screen's own data behaves exactly as it does on the device: MenuDataSource
   reads the API and falls back to seed data when it cannot be reached, and says
   which of the two is on screen. *@

<PageTitle>Terminal preview</PageTitle>

<section class="panel tp">

    <header class="tp__head">
        <p class="tp__kicker">DEVELOPMENT PREVIEW &middot; MAUI SCREEN</p>
        <h1 class="tp__title">Terminal &middot; Order entry</h1>
        <p class="tp__note">
            The same <code>TerminalShell</code> and <code>OrderEntryScreen</code> components
            <code>Restaurant.Mobile</code> renders, stood up at the terminal's 1440&times;900 so the
            screen can be worked on without an emulator. This route is not a back-office
            destination and ships to no venue. Hover lifts are retracted inside the frame, because
            the terminal has no hover.
        </p>
    </header>

    <p class="tp__kicker">1440 &times; 900 &middot; the primary design</p>

    <div class="tp__frame">
        <div class="tp__device">
            <TerminalShell ActiveDestination="order">
                <OrderEntryScreen CustomerName="Mobile User" TableId="1" />
            </TerminalShell>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* The handheld step, stood up beside the design it degrades from.

       Both frames are on this page at once and both are correct at once, which
       is the whole reason the step is a container query on the shell rather
       than a media query on the viewport: the rule asks how wide the shell is,
       and these two shells are 1440px and 393px in the same browser window. A
       viewport rule would flip both frames together the moment the window
       narrowed, and would report the opposite of the truth about the device.

       393x785 is what the Datalogic Memor 20's WebView reports for its
       1080x2160 screen at 440dpi, now that MainActivity hides both of Android's
       system bars: the terminal owns the whole panel, so the WebView is the
       whole panel. The number has moved twice. It read 393x785 while the window
       drew edge to edge underneath the bars, and that 785 was a defect — the
       shell was measuring 72px of chrome it did not own and was clipped at both
       ends. It read 393x713 while the window was fitted to them — 2160 less a
       66px status bar and a 132px navigation bar is 1962 device pixels, which is
       713 CSS pixels — which was correct for a terminal that shares its screen
       with the host. It reads 393x785 again now, the same figure as the defect,
       reached by removing the bars rather than by ignoring them. It is a real
       measurement off a real device at every step, not a chosen number — and
       Part II-A · Handheld is provisional for exactly that reason: the device is
       one candidate and the fleet is not settled. *@
    <p class="tp__kicker">393 &times; 785 &middot; handheld &middot; provisional</p>

    <div class="tp__frame tp__frame--handheld">
        <div class="tp__device tp__device--handheld">
            <TerminalShell ActiveDestination="order">
                <OrderEntryScreen CustomerName="Mobile User" TableId="1" />
            </TerminalShell>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
